[ Lithium fluoride ](/wiki/Lithium_fluoride "Lithium fluoride") , artificially
grown as [ crystal ](/wiki/Crystal "Crystal") , is clear and transparent and
often used in specialist optics for [ IR ](/wiki/Infrared "Infrared") , [ UV
](/wiki/Ultraviolet "Ultraviolet") and VUV ( [ vacuum UV ](/wiki/Vacuum_UV
"Vacuum UV") ) applications. It has one of the lowest [ refractive indices
](/wiki/Refractive_index "Refractive index") and the furthest transmission
range in the deep UV of most common materials.  [174]  Finely divided lithium
fluoride powder has been used for [ thermoluminescent radiation dosimetry
](/wiki/Thermoluminescent_Dosimeter "Thermoluminescent Dosimeter") (TLD): when
a sample of such is exposed to radiation, it accumulates [ crystal defects
](/wiki/Crystal_defect "Crystal defect") which, when heated, resolve via a
release of bluish light whose intensity is proportional to the [ absorbed dose
](/wiki/Absorbed_dose "Absorbed dose") , thus allowing this to be quantified.
[175]  Lithium fluoride is sometimes used in focal lenses of [ telescopes
](/wiki/Telescope "Telescope") .  [20]  [176]

