Many of the characteristics of seawater correspond to those of  [ water
](https://www.britannica.com/science/water) in general, because of their
common chemical and physical properties. For example, the molecular structure
of seawater, like that of fresh water, favours the formation of bonds among
molecules. Some of the distinctive qualities of seawater are attributable to
its salt content. The  [ viscosity
](https://www.britannica.com/science/viscosity) (i.e., internal resistance to
flow) of seawater, for example, is higher than that of fresh water because of
its higher salinity. The [ density
](https://www.britannica.com/science/density) of seawater also is higher for
the same reason. Seawater’s [ freezing point
](https://www.britannica.com/science/freezing-point) is lower than that of
pure water, and its [ boiling point
](https://www.britannica.com/science/boiling-point) is higher.

